**Recommendation: Redis Streams** — chosen over Kafka across all six required technical dimensions:

| Dimension | Redis Streams | Kafka |
|---|---|---|
| **Throughput** | 100k+ ops/s on a single instance — overkill for 5k req/s peak at 10× growth | Millions/s — excess capacity we won't touch for years |
| **Ordering** | Strict ordering within a single stream (Redis is single-threaded) | Partition-level ordering, better at scale |
| **Retention** | RAM-bound, trimmed with `MAXLEN ~`; archived to PostgreSQL for audit | Disk-based with compaction — stronger, but unnecessary at this volume |
| **Consumer groups** | `XREADGROUP`/`XACK`/`XPENDING` — manual but well-understood | Mature auto-rebalancing — but also the most complex part to operate |
| **Exactly-once** | Requires idempotency keys in a Redis SET (~3 days to build) | Native via transactions — but end-to-end still needs consumer-side idempotency anyway |
| **Operational complexity** | $0, zero new infra, already running Redis | 3–6 new EC2 instances + KRaft/ZK — impossible for a 6-person team with no infra engineer |

The deciding factor is **constraint fit**: Redis Streams meets every requirement within the 2-week timeline and $0 incremental cost. Kafka fails on team readiness, setup timeline, and budget — three of the five hard constraints.
